{-
   Constant folding **at the type's own width**, which is the one thing a folder can get wrong in a
   way no golden shows.

   Every case here is arithmetic over literals, so the resolve stage answers it in its optimizer
   rather than at run time, and every case is at a width **narrower than the register holding it**.
   That is the whole point: `200 + 100` is 300 in a thirty-two bit register and 44 in a `U8`, and a
   folder that computes the first and stores it as the second has produced a number the machine
   never would. `.run.expect` was once shared between two resolve stages folding through different
   code, so it asserted that both agreed with each other and with the machine.

   The `-no-ir-opt` comparison the drivers make is the other half: with the optimizer off, none of
   this folds and the answers come from `lower/width.cpp`'s masks. A fold at the wrong width shows
   up there as two different numbers for one program. The `.jsir.js.expect` beside this file is a
   third reading of the same claim - `codegen/js` masks narrow integers itself and folds the whole
   of `main` to 255 - which costs a golden and covers the one target neither of the other two is.

   Six of the eight cases discriminate on their own - fold them at thirty-two bits and `main` is not
   255. The two refinement cases do not, because a value on its way into a field is narrowed by the
   store whether or not anything folded it; what they assert is that the refinement's own width is
   what the fold used, which is visible in `.ssa.opt.expect` as the answer rather than as the
   arithmetic. That was the case the two stages disagreed about *who* computes: the shipping stage
   declined refinement arithmetic outright, because its two backends spelled the width differently,
   and this stage has one backend and `resolve_ssa/lower/width.cpp` states the width - see the note
   in `resolve_ssa/opt/fold.cpp`.

   Each case contributes a distinct power of two and `main` is 255 when they all pass.
-}

fn check(ok: Bool, weight: Int) -> Int = if ok then weight else 0

-- An unsigned narrow type, wrapping. 300 is not a `U8`, and 44 is what one holds.
fn unsignedWrap() -> Int = check((200 :: U8) + 100 == 44, 1)

-- The signed half, whose wrap goes the other way: 200 has the eighth bit set, so an `I8` reads it as
-- -56. Written as `0 - 56` rather than with a unary minus, which the shipping stage typed as an
-- `I32` whatever the position wanted.
fn signedWrap() -> Int = check((100 :: I8) + 100 == (0 :: I8) - 56, 2)

-- A shift is `Wraps` in `op.def` for exactly this reason: the bits it moves out of the declared width
-- are gone, and 400 masked to eight is 144.
fn narrowShift() -> Int = check((200 :: U8) `shl` 1 == 144, 4)

{-
   The two refinements, reached the way `doc/spec/repr.md` says one is: a value at the natural width
   converted on its way into the field, and widened back on the way out.

   `@bits(4) U32` keeps four bits of 300, which is 12. `@bits(5) I32` keeps five of 20 - and the fifth
   is the sign, so what comes back out is -12 rather than 20. The signed one is the case that says the
   fold sign-extends at the refinement's width rather than masking at it.
-}
alias Small = @bits(4) U32
alias SmallSigned = @bits(5) I32

data Packed {small: Small, signed: SmallSigned}

fn packed() -> Packed = Packed {small: (250 :: U32) + 50, signed: (10 :: I32) + 10}

fn refinedUnsigned() -> Int = check(packed().small == 12, 8)

fn refinedSigned() -> Int = check(packed().signed == (0 :: I32) - 12, 16)

{-
   The two narrow comparisons, which are the same eight bits ordered two ways.

   A comparison happens at the *operands'* signedness and not at its own result type, and each of
   these is false under the other reading: `-1 < 0` is true signed and false unsigned, and `255 > 1`
   is true unsigned and false signed. A folder that took the signedness off the `Bool` it answers
   would fail both.
-}
fn signedCompare() -> Int = check((0 :: I8) - 1 < (0 :: I8), 32)

fn unsignedCompare() -> Int = check((255 :: U8) > (1 :: U8), 64)

-- And a complement, which is the one bitwise operation that takes an in-range operand out of range:
-- every bit of a `U8` zero is set, and the value that is, is 255 rather than the register's own
-- thirty-two ones.
fn narrowComplement() -> Int = check(not(0 :: U8) == 255, 128)

fn main() -> Int =
    unsignedWrap() + signedWrap() + narrowShift() +
    refinedUnsigned() + refinedSigned() +
    signedCompare() + unsignedCompare() + narrowComplement()
